# Local Maintainer Defaults

Template for `LOCAL.md`. Copy it, fill it in, and keep it untracked:

```bash
cp LOCAL.md.example LOCAL.md
```

`LOCAL.md` records *one maintainer's environment* — the concrete commands and
paths an agent cannot guess from the package repository alone. `SKILL.md` and
the `references/` stay neutral so they can be shared; everything personal
belongs here. `.gitignore` keeps `LOCAL.md` out of git so forks do not carry
each other's build environment.

Delete the sections that do not apply. Keep entries short and concrete: exact
commands, exact paths. Prefer stating what an agent would otherwise get wrong.

## Builder

Name the builder and give the exact invocation, because the skill deliberately
does not assume one. Common shapes:

```bash
# gbp driving pbuilder
gbp buildpackage --git-pbuilder --git-arch=<arch> --git-dist=<suite>

# sbuild
sbuild -d <suite> --arch=<arch>

# pbuilder directly, from a source package
pbuilder build ../<source>_<version>.dsc

# a personal wrapper script
<wrapper> <suite> <arch> build ../<source>_<version>.dsc
```

Record the things that change follow-up commands:

- Where `.changes`, `.deb`, and `.buildinfo` files land (`..` for
  `dpkg-buildpackage`/gbp, a per-suite result directory for most chroot
  builders). `lintian`, `debdiff`, `debc`, and `dput` all need this path.
- Whether the builder writes its own log, and where. If it does, do not wrap
  it in an extra `tee`.
- Which operations need `sudo`, network, or writes outside the workspace
  (typically chroot create/update) so they can be confirmed first.

## Default architecture and suite

- Architecture: `<arch>` (e.g. `amd64`).
- Suite: `<suite>` (e.g. `sid` — equivalent to `unstable`; preserve the
  spelling actually used in commands when documenting or rerunning).

## Chroot and mirror configuration

- Chroot names/locations, and how they are created and refreshed.
- The mirror or caching proxy these chroots use, if it is not the system
  default.

## gbp usage

State which parts of the workflow go through `gbp` (`gbp dch`,
`gbp import-orig`, `gbp pq import/export`, `gbp tag`) and, if the builder is
not `gbp buildpackage`, say so explicitly so an agent does not fall back to it.

## Commit and release-changelog timing

Record when the release changelog entry is committed: before the upload, or
delayed until it succeeds. The mechanics — and the `--git-ignore-new` build a
delayed commit implies — are in `references/workflows.md` (*Commit Before
Changelog Generation*); this file only records which rule applies here.

## Upload targets

- QA / intermediate uploads: e.g. `dput debusine.debian.net <changes>`.
- Archive uploads: name the profile, and whether an agent may ever run it
  without an explicit instruction (usually not).

Generic `dput` safety rules live in `references/tools.md` (Upload Helpers) and
the archive-vs-debusine distinction in `references/services.md`; this file only
records *which* targets are used here.

## Anything else an agent would guess wrong

Signing keys, identity used for NMUs, packages with unusual local conventions,
directories to stay out of, network restrictions.
